n't i have  to confess but maybe some of you were  hanging out  and xi jinping  gave a speech there which is all about  how china was a  globalizing outward looking power that  would in many ways look to uphold the  pillars of international order now very  much he made that speech in january 2017  a time when a gentleman named donald j  trump was about to be inaugurated as  president of the united states so i  think that while he never mentioned his  name most of president xi's comments  were really uh giving the nod to saying  i believe in globalization i believe in  openness unlike that guy in washington  uh and he said something not dissimilar  actually this year virtually at davos in  2021 but of course um i gather that  davos this year was not in fact um held  in  in person i mentioned that because there  we have um at the top level there the um  great hall of the people  and that is of course this hugely  symbolic place where um actually the uh  the great meetings the congresses all of  that uh in the chinese communist  calendar are held over the months years  and decades and that's very different xi  jinping not different person of course  but different message much more about  china's place in the world seeing china  as a place that in some ways is not  globalized and open and all the stuff  that was said at davos but under siege  in many ways trying to be  fighting back against containment from  the united states from the western world  from powers who basically want to see  china fail and  he in this version of the narrative is  about a china which is looking to use  its economic power its military power  its cultural power to push back and  create an alternative chinese uh vision  of course of its place in wider society  but certainly one that is highly  orchestrated and stabilized at home so  inward looking  communist part of china outward looking  communist part of china  different locations beijing and davos  but it's not all about the leader  let me use this if i may to put forward  a little mnemonic which i have written  about and if any of you uh want to look  on online in the journal foreign affairs  i had a piece back in january of this  year which talks about this in more  detail but let me give you a quick  summary of why i think  what's on here on this slide is a useful  way to try and understand the complexity  of china today and i've called it the  china dna model  it's not just supposed to be a kind of  conventional wisdom or cliche because we  said that everything has you know dna  you know asking tough questions is in  the dna of guests at the how light gets  in festival that sort of thing but i  mean something's more specific do we  have any nobel prize-winning biologists  in the audience today okay well that's  good you don't have to be a developer  president to know that if you look at  natural molecule of dna it's made up of  four nucleotides which have the initials  of the chemicals acgt and i've realized  actually that those dna nucleotide  initials are the same as the factors  that i think make up today's china  and they are  authoritarianism consumerism  globalization and technology what do i  mean by those terms authoritarianism  china today  actually really through most of its  communist period but much more  explicitly today has no embarrassment  no kind of awkwardness about saying we  run a different sort of political system  we are we're not a liberal democracy  we're not looking to become a liberal  democracy you can say this is good you  can say this is bad i'm just saying  analytically this is the way that china  is presenting itself these days it does  sometimes make your gesture saying we're  a different type of democracy but that's  a bit like those people who send you  those burgers i think which they say  tastes just like meat i mean that's like  meat as long as it tastes like cabbage  but authoritarianism i think is just a  straightforward actually quite  analytical way to look 